//! lotus/stereo_dc_remove.rye -- ALES155: the DC-offset remover carried into stereo, TWO INDEPENDENT MEANS taken back out, a per-channel PURE TRANSLATION that PRESERVES the stereo image. ALES90 named `dc_remove`:
//! subtract a span's mean from every sample, y = saturate(x − mean), the block-mean remover every chain carries right after a nonlinearity (a rectifier or asymmetric drive shifts the mean off zero, and a DC
//! offset eats headroom and thumps at a cut seam). That is a mono edit. Yet a Lotus master is a StereoClip (ALES10), two Clips whose defining invariant is that LEFT and RIGHT hold the SAME length -- and the two
//! channels carry their OWN offsets: an even map or an asymmetric clip can bias the left and the right by DIFFERENT amounts. So this rung removes each channel's own mean INDEPENDENTLY: run ALES90's proven mono
//! `dc_remove` on each channel over the SAME span, the left correcting the left's offset and the right the right's.
//!
//! The rung is the honest OPPOSITE of the stereo rectifier (ALES138). The rectifier is an EVEN map that COLLAPSES the stereo image (an out-of-phase master folds to mono); DC removal is a per-channel PURE
//! TRANSLATION, and a translation PRESERVES every sample-to-sample difference, so it PRESERVES the image: an out-of-phase master (right = −left) that rides a common-mode DC offset comes back STILL out of phase
//! (each channel's own mean removed, right = −left again), the antisymmetry kept rather than collapsed. The stereo lift is length-preserving, so it meets NO desync wall (unlike the silence edits ALES148-154): the
//! two channels simply run their own removal, each landing its own mean under one LSB, and the balance invariant is never at risk because no length changes.
//!
//! The rule, stated once: refuse BadRange (a span outside the samples) BEFORE either channel is mutated -- the check depends only on the shared length, so it is made ONCE up front, and a refusal never DC-removes one
//! channel and leaves the other biased; otherwise `dc_remove` both channels over [start, start+count) via ALES90. DcError reused whole -- the stereo lift adds no fault (the operation takes no coefficient, only a span).
//!
//! The crux -- five laws. THE STEREO DC-REMOVE LAW: each channel equals ALES90's mono `dc_remove` over the same span byte for byte -- each channel's own truncated mean subtracted, saturating once per sample at the
//! rail. THE INDEPENDENT-OFFSET LAW (the stereo crux): two channels carrying DIFFERENT DC offsets each land under one LSB INDEPENDENTLY -- the left's offset never leaks into the right, and a channel already zero-mean
//! is unchanged while its partner is corrected. THE IMAGE-PRESERVE LAW: because the map is a per-channel translation, an out-of-phase master riding a common-mode offset returns STILL out of phase (the honest
//! opposite of the rectifier's collapse), a both-zero-mean master is unchanged exactly, and the remove is idempotent where nothing saturates. THE COMPOSITION LAW: it undoes the stereo rectifier's offset -- a
//! symmetric master rectified through ALES138 rides a positive DC on BOTH channels, and stereo_dc_remove restores a near-zero mean on each. THE BALANCE / ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: left.len == right.len after (a
//! remove writes values only), a refusal leaves BOTH channels byte for byte untouched and still balanced, count = 0 and the empty span the identity on both.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus - the creative suite) - waymark ALES - rung ALES155.
//! Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-060518_fill-ales155-lotus-stereo-dc-remove.md
//! Stands on: lotus/dc_remove.rye (ALES90) -- the mono block-mean DC remover run once per channel, its `dc_remove` and `DcError` reused whole; lotus/stereo_rectify.rye (ALES138) -- the EVEN map whose per-channel DC
//! offset this rung undoes (the composition proof) and whose image-COLLAPSE this rung's image-PRESERVE answers; lotus/rectify.rye (ALES84) -- the mono rectifier underneath ALES138; lotus/pan.rye (ALES10) -- the
//! StereoClip (two mono Clips, left/right) and its balance invariant; lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) -- the Clip, its buf/len/samples, sample_min/max, and the one true saturate reused through ALES90.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE -- software only, purely LOCAL. Two bounded in-process i16 Clips (left, right) on one bench, siloed to lotus/. It changes sample VALUES only through ALES90's own dc_remove -- a two-pass block statistic
//! per channel (sum in i64, then subtract, one saturate per sample), memoryless (no carried state, no feedback, no delay line), never a length. The "DC" is the arithmetic mean of the block, the zero-frequency
//! component by definition, not a claim about any hardware highpass; no real sample rate, no anti-aliasing, no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real speaker.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/stereo_dc_remove.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const pan = @import("pan.rye");
const dc_remove = @import("dc_remove.rye");

pub const stereo_dc_remove_version = "20260815.060518";

/// Stereo DC-remove's faults are exactly ALES90's own `DcError` -- `BadRange` (a span outside the samples) -- reused by name, since the stereo lift adds no new fault: it runs the same removal on each channel after
/// one shared validation. The remover names no other fault (it takes no gain and no threshold, only a span).
pub const StereoDcError = dc_remove.DcError;

/// Remove the DC offset from `[start, start+count)` in BOTH channels of `sc`, each channel's OWN mean subtracted, running ALES90's proven mono `dc_remove`. Each sample becomes `saturate(x − mean_of_its_channel)` -- a
/// per-channel pure translation that lands each channel's span average on zero. The two channels carry independent offsets, so each removes its own: the left's mean never touches the right. Validates the span once
/// against the shared length before either channel is mutated: refuses `BadRange`, both channels untouched. Values only -- never the length; the two channels leave balanced, each with residual mean magnitude under
/// one LSB where nothing saturates. An EMPTY span (count == 0) is a clean no-op on both.
pub fn stereo_dc_remove(sc: *pan.StereoClip, start: u32, count: u32) StereoDcError!void {
    // invariant: the two channels enter balanced -- the defining StereoClip invariant, the ground the edit preserves.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);

    // invariant: the span is legal -- refused by name before any write, so a refusal never DC-removes one channel and leaves the other biased. The span check depends only on the shared length, made once up front; it
    // mirrors ALES90's own guard exactly, so each mono removal below is pre-validated to succeed. The remover carries no other parameter, so this lone check is the whole gate.
    const len: u32 = sc.left.len;
    if (start > len) return error.BadRange;
    if (count > len - start) return error.BadRange;

    // Every check passed and holds identically for both channels, so each mono dc_remove is pre-validated to succeed -- no refusal can land between the two channel edits. Each channel's OWN mean is subtracted.
    try dc_remove.dc_remove(&sc.left, start, count);
    try dc_remove.dc_remove(&sc.right, start, count);

    // invariant: the channels leave balanced -- a DC remove writes values only, so each channel holds its starting length and the stereo image stays aligned in time.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);
    assert(sc.left.len <= sc.left.buf.len and sc.right.len <= sc.right.buf.len);
}

// --- selftest: THE STEREO DC-REMOVE LAW -- each channel equals ALES90's mono dc_remove over the same span byte for byte; THE INDEPENDENT-OFFSET LAW -- two channels carrying DIFFERENT offsets each land under one LSB
//     independently, a zero-mean channel unchanged while its partner is corrected; THE IMAGE-PRESERVE LAW -- an out-of-phase master riding a common offset returns STILL out of phase (the honest opposite of the
//     rectifier's collapse), a both-zero-mean master unchanged, the map idempotent; THE COMPOSITION LAW -- it undoes the stereo rectifier's per-channel offset; THE BALANCE / ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW -- length held,
//     a refusal leaving both channels untouched and balanced, count = 0 and the empty span the identity ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_stereo(sc: *pan.StereoClip, l: []const i16, r: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(&sc.left, 0, l);
    try timeline.splice(&sc.right, 0, r);
}

fn span_sum(s: []const i16) i64 {
    var sum: i64 = 0;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < s.len) : (i += 1) sum += @as(i64, s[i]);
    return sum;
}

fn abs_i64(v: i64) i64 {
    return if (v < 0) -v else v;
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    const stereo_rectify = @import("stereo_rectify.rye"); // ALES138 — the EVEN map whose per-channel offset this rung undoes, and whose image-collapse this rung's image-preserve answers

    // 1. THE STEREO DC-REMOVE LAW -- each channel equals ALES90's mono dc_remove over the same span, proven side by side with DIFFERENT per-channel offsets: a left biased +1100, a right biased −500.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 1000, 1200, 900, 1300 }, &[_]i16{ -400, -600, -300, -700 });
        try stereo_dc_remove(&sc, 0, 4);

        var ml = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&ml, 0, &[_]i16{ 1000, 1200, 900, 1300 });
        try dc_remove.dc_remove(&ml, 0, 4);
        var mr = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&mr, 0, &[_]i16{ -400, -600, -300, -700 });
        try dc_remove.dc_remove(&mr, 0, 4);

        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), ml.samples())) return error.LeftChannelWrong;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), mr.samples())) return error.RightChannelWrong;
        // by hand: left mean 1100 -> {−100, 100, −200, 200}; right mean −500 -> {100, −100, 200, −200}.
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ -100, 100, -200, 200 })) return error.LeftHandWrong;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 100, -100, 200, -200 })) return error.RightHandWrong;
    }

    // 2. THE INDEPENDENT-OFFSET LAW -- the stereo crux. Two channels carrying DIFFERENT offsets each land under one LSB after removal, independently; the left's large offset never leaks into the right's residual, and
    //    the right's small offset never inflates the left's. Both residual sums strictly below the count.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 8000, 8100, 7900, 8050, 8000, 7950 }, &[_]i16{ -30, 40, -20, 25, -35, 20 });
        try stereo_dc_remove(&sc, 0, 6);
        if (abs_i64(span_sum(sc.left.samples())) >= 6) return error.LeftResidualUnbounded; // the big left offset removed to under one LSB
        if (abs_i64(span_sum(sc.right.samples())) >= 6) return error.RightResidualUnbounded; // the small right offset removed to under one LSB
    }

    // 2b. A ZERO-MEAN CHANNEL IS UNCHANGED WHILE ITS PARTNER IS CORRECTED -- the left already centers on zero (mean 0, nothing subtracted), the right rides a +600 offset; removal touches only the right.
    {
        const centered = [_]i16{ 100, -100, 200, -200 };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &centered, &[_]i16{ 500, 700, 400, 800 }); // right mean 600
        try stereo_dc_remove(&sc, 0, 4);
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &centered)) return error.ZeroMeanLeftChanged; // the left passed byte for byte
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ -100, 100, -200, 200 })) return error.PartnerRightWrong; // the right corrected
    }

    // 3. THE IMAGE-PRESERVE LAW -- the honest opposite of the rectifier (ALES138), which COLLAPSES an out-of-phase master to mono. DC removal is a per-channel PURE TRANSLATION, so it PRESERVES the image: an out-of-
    //    phase master (right = −left AC) riding a common-mode +1000 offset comes back STILL out of phase (each channel's own mean removed), right = −left at every index, the antisymmetry KEPT.
    {
        // left = 1000 + w, right = 1000 − w, with w = {200, −300, 500, −400} (zero-mean AC). Both channels' mean is 1000.
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 1200, 700, 1500, 600 }, &[_]i16{ 800, 1300, 500, 1400 });
        try stereo_dc_remove(&sc, 0, 4);
        const l = sc.left.samples();
        const r = sc.right.samples();
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < 4) : (i += 1) {
            if (@as(i32, r[i]) != -@as(i32, l[i])) return error.ImageNotPreserved; // still exactly out of phase — the translation kept the antisymmetry
        }
        if (!eq(l, &[_]i16{ 200, -300, 500, -400 })) return error.ImagePreserveMagnitudeWrong;
    }

    // 3b. A BOTH-ZERO-MEAN MASTER IS UNCHANGED EXACTLY, AND THE MAP IS IDEMPOTENT where nothing saturates -- a master already centered on both channels passes byte for byte, and removing a mid-level master twice
    //     equals removing it once.
    {
        const zl = [_]i16{ 300, -300, 600, -600 };
        const zr = [_]i16{ -600, 600, -300, 300 };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &zl, &zr);
        try stereo_dc_remove(&sc, 0, 4);
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &zl) or !eq(sc.right.samples(), &zr)) return error.ZeroMeanMasterChanged;

        const ml_sig = [_]i16{ 4000, 4200, 3900, 4300, 4100, 3950 };
        const mr_sig = [_]i16{ -2000, -1800, -2100, -1700, -1900, -2050 };
        var once = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&once, &ml_sig, &mr_sig);
        try stereo_dc_remove(&once, 0, 6);
        var twice = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&twice, &ml_sig, &mr_sig);
        try stereo_dc_remove(&twice, 0, 6);
        try stereo_dc_remove(&twice, 0, 6);
        if (!eq(once.left.samples(), twice.left.samples())) return error.LeftNotIdempotent;
        if (!eq(once.right.samples(), twice.right.samples())) return error.RightNotIdempotent;
    }

    // 4. THE COMPOSITION LAW -- the reason this rung exists, read in stereo. A symmetric zero-mean master full-wave-rectified through ALES138 turns every sample positive on BOTH channels, each riding a large positive
    //    DC offset; stereo_dc_remove restores a near-zero mean on each. Prove the whole road: the rectified means are large and positive on both, the DC-removed residuals under one LSB on both.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 3000, -3000, 3000, -3000, 3000, -3000 }, &[_]i16{ -2000, 2000, -2000, 2000, -2000, 2000 });
        try stereo_rectify.stereo_rectify(&sc, 0, 6); // both channels now one-sided: left all 3000 (sum 18000), right all 2000 (sum 12000)
        if (span_sum(sc.left.samples()) != 18000) return error.RectifyLeftAssumptionWrong; // the left offset the rung must undo is really there
        if (span_sum(sc.right.samples()) != 12000) return error.RectifyRightAssumptionWrong; // the right offset too
        try stereo_dc_remove(&sc, 0, 6); // each channel's own mean removed
        if (abs_i64(span_sum(sc.left.samples())) >= 6) return error.RectLeftOffsetNotRemoved;
        if (abs_i64(span_sum(sc.right.samples())) >= 6) return error.RectRightOffsetNotRemoved;
    }

    // 5. THE SPAN DISCIPLINE -- DC-removing a sub-span leaves the samples outside [start, count) exactly as they were on both channels, and removes only the inner span's mean per channel. Remove the middle two of six
    //    on both; the left inner {5000, 5000} has mean 5000 -> both drop to 0, the right inner {−800, −800} has mean −800 -> both to 0; the outer four untouched on each.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 100, 200, 5000, 5000, 300, 400 }, &[_]i16{ -10, -20, -800, -800, -30, -40 });
        try stereo_dc_remove(&sc, 2, 2); // indices 2,3 only, both channels
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 100, 200, 0, 0, 300, 400 })) return error.LeftSpanLeaked;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ -10, -20, 0, 0, -30, -40 })) return error.RightSpanLeaked;
    }

    // 6a. THE BALANCE / LENGTH LAW -- the channels stay equal length and each holds its starting length; a DC remove writes values only.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 1000, 1200, 900, 1300, 1100 }, &[_]i16{ -500, -400, -600, -300, -700 });
        try stereo_dc_remove(&sc, 0, 5);
        if (sc.left.len != sc.right.len) return error.Unbalanced;
        if (sc.left.len != 5) return error.LengthChanged;
    }

    // 6b. THE ATOMICITY LAW, bad span -- a span past the samples refuses BadRange, both channels byte for byte untouched and still balanced. The remover names no other fault (it takes no coefficient), so this lone
    //     refusal is the whole atomicity story.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 5, -6, 7 }, &[_]i16{ -7, 6, -5 });
        if (stereo_dc_remove(&sc, 1, 3)) |_| return error.BadRangeSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongBadRange;
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 5, -6, 7 })) return error.BadRangeLeftMutated;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ -7, 6, -5 })) return error.BadRangeRightMutated;
        if (sc.left.len != sc.right.len) return error.BadRangeUnbalanced;
    }

    // 6c. THE DEGENERATE LAW -- count = 0 (an empty span in the middle) is the identity on both channels; no mean to remove and no divide by zero.
    {
        var noop = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&noop, &[_]i16{ 700, -700, 1400 }, &[_]i16{ -1400, 700, -700 });
        try stereo_dc_remove(&noop, 1, 0); // empty span — identity
        if (!eq(noop.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 700, -700, 1400 })) return error.ZeroCountLeftChanged;
        if (!eq(noop.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ -1400, 700, -700 })) return error.ZeroCountRightChanged;
    }

    print("GREEN ales-stereo-dc-remove: the DC-OFFSET REMOVER CARRIED INTO STEREO, two independent means taken back out, a per-channel PURE TRANSLATION that PRESERVES the stereo image — remove the DC offset from a " ++
        "span in both channels of a master, running ALES90's proven mono dc_remove on each (y = saturate(x − mean), the block-mean remover every chain carries right after a nonlinearity). A Lotus master is a " ++
        "StereoClip (ALES10) whose defining invariant is that left and right hold the same length, and the two channels carry their OWN offsets — an even map or asymmetric clip can bias them by different amounts — " ++
        "so this rung removes each channel's own mean INDEPENDENTLY, the left correcting the left and the right the right. It is the honest OPPOSITE of the stereo rectifier (ALES138): the rectifier is an EVEN map " ++
        "that COLLAPSES an out-of-phase master to mono, where DC removal is a per-channel translation that PRESERVES every sample-to-sample difference, so an out-of-phase master riding a common-mode offset comes " ++
        "back STILL out of phase, the antisymmetry kept. The lift is length-preserving, so it meets no desync wall (unlike the silence edits ALES148 through ALES154): each channel simply runs its own removal, and " ++
        "the balance invariant is never at risk. It validates the span once against the shared length before either channel is mutated (BadRange on a span past the samples), so a refusal never DC-removes one channel " ++
        "and leaves the other biased; DcError reused whole. The crux is five laws. THE STEREO DC-REMOVE LAW: each channel equals ALES90's mono dc_remove over the same span byte for byte. THE INDEPENDENT-OFFSET LAW: " ++
        "two channels carrying different offsets each land under one LSB independently, the left's offset never leaking into the right, a zero-mean channel unchanged while its partner is corrected. THE IMAGE-PRESERVE " ++
        "LAW: an out-of-phase master riding a common offset returns still out of phase (the opposite of the rectifier's collapse), a both-zero-mean master unchanged exactly, the map idempotent where nothing " ++
        "saturates. THE COMPOSITION LAW: it undoes the stereo rectifier's per-channel offset (a symmetric master rectified through ALES138 rides a positive DC on both channels; stereo_dc_remove restores a near-zero " ++
        "mean on each). THE BALANCE / ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: left.len == right.len after, a refusal leaves both channels untouched and balanced, count = 0 and the empty span the identity on both. Purely local " ++
        "— two bounded i16 Clips DC-removed through the proven mono remover, a two-pass block statistic per channel (sum in i64, then subtract, one saturate per sample), memoryless, never a length; no real sample " ++
        "rate, no anti-aliasing, no network, no keys, no funds, no real speaker\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
